kube api server is the primary management component in kubernetes

when you run kubectl command, it is reaching to the kubeapiserver
the kubeapiserver first authnticate the request and validates it, then retreives data from etcd xluster and responds back with the requested info

you dont need to use kubectl all the time, instaed you an also invoke its API, by sending a post request

kubeapiserver is the only component that interact with etcd data store

--
if you creating cluster from scratch(manual) way
then kubeapiserver is available as binary in kubernetes release page
download it and configure it to run as a service on kubernetes master node

kube-apiserver.service
>kube-apiserver runs with lot of parameters, once you install it you will see it

you can view the kubeapiserver service in 
cat /etc/systemd/system/kube-apiserver.service
whole apiserver configuration, its infra everything will be mentioned in this service file
---
if you bootstrap(create) your cluster using kubeadmin tool
the apiserver will deployed as pods in kube system namespace on master node
kubectl get pods -n kube-system

the apiserver will be created as yaml file in
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
whole apiserver configuration, its infra everything will be mentioned in this yaml file

----

you can also see running process for kube-apiserver using
ps -aux | grep kube-apiserver